{
  "sn41.5:0.1": "Linked Discourses 41.5 ",
  "sn41.5:0.2": "1. With Citta ",
  "sn41.5:0.3": "With Kāmabhū (1st) ",
  "sn41.5:1.1": "At one time Venerable Kāmabhū was staying near Macchikāsaṇḍa in the Wild Mango Grove. ",
  "sn41.5:1.2": "Then Citta the householder went up to Venerable Kāmabhū, bowed, and sat down to one side. Kāmabhū said to him, ",
  "sn41.5:2.1": "“Householder, there is this saying: ",
  "sn41.5:3.1": "‘With faultless parts and white canopy, ",
  "sn41.5:3.2": "the one-spoked chariot rolls on. ",
  "sn41.5:3.3": "See it come, untroubled, ",
  "sn41.5:3.4": "with stream cut, unbound.’ ",
  "sn41.5:4.1": "How should we see the detailed meaning of this brief statement?” ",
  "sn41.5:4.2": "“Sir, was this spoken by the Buddha?” ",
  "sn41.5:4.3": "“Yes, householder.” ",
  "sn41.5:4.4": "“Well then, sir, please wait a while as I consider the meaning of this.” ",
  "sn41.5:4.5": "Then after staying silent for a while, Citta said to Kāmabhū: ",
  "sn41.5:5.1": "“Sir, ‘faultless parts’ is a term for ethics. ",
  "sn41.5:5.2": "‘White canopy’ is a term for freedom. ",
  "sn41.5:5.3": "‘One spoke’ is a term for mindfulness. ",
  "sn41.5:5.4": "‘Rolls on’ is a term for going forward and coming back. ",
  "sn41.5:5.5": "‘Chariot’ is a term for this body made up of the four principal states, produced by mother and father, built up from rice and porridge, liable to impermanence, to wearing away and erosion, to breaking up and destruction. ",
  "sn